1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:07,069
I am the artist. I turn your results into figures, reports and slides that make the science clear.

2
00:00:07,450 --> 00:00:20,206
I draw publication figures, from model-accuracy curves to heatmaps and volcano plots, each one labelled, with proper axes and legends. I build slide decks, and keep a project report of the figures so far.

3
00:00:20,206 --> 00:00:27,782
What makes me different: I draw each figure from your real data and save it at print resolution.

4
00:00:28,199 --> 00:00:37,287
I cover figures, reports and slides. I draw the numbers as they arrive, from your results or an analysis already run; I compute none myself.

5
00:00:37,287 --> 00:00:44,306
I never invent a data point to fill a plot; when an input is missing, I say so.

6
00:00:44,699 --> 00:00:56,249
A real run, on simulated data. The member asks me for one figure: colony size by growth medium, every point shown, the means marked.

7
00:00:56,249 --> 00:01:05,468
My answer is Rendered. The image itself says the data are simulated, the caption says what to notice, and I state that I ran no test.

8
00:01:05,849 --> 00:01:14,101
Say what you want and where it is going: a figure sized for a journal, a deck for lab meeting, or a new page in the project report.

9
00:01:14,101 --> 00:01:22,017
Name your colours and figure size in the request; within a project I keep one palette for every figure.

10
00:01:22,399 --> 00:01:33,484
To use me, type to Claude Code and name me: ask the artist for a figure from your results, and say what it is for. A figure for a talk differs from one for a paper.

11
00:01:33,484 --> 00:01:42,564
My reply starts with Rendered, Skipped or Failed. Then the figure, and a caption that says what to notice.
